### What is the ceiling of a number?

The ceiling of a key in a sorted array is the smallest element that is greater than or equal to the key. If the key itself exists in the array, it is its own ceiling. If the key is larger than every element, no ceiling exists.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class CeilingOfNumber {

    public static int findCeiling(int[] arr, int key) {
        if (key > arr[arr.length - 1]) return -1;

        int start = 0, end = arr.length - 1;
        while (start <= end) {
            int mid = start + (end - start) / 2;
            if (arr[mid] == key) return mid;
            if (key < arr[mid]) end = mid - 1;
            else start = mid + 1;
        }
        return start;  // start points to the ceiling
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] slabs = {1000, 3000, 8000, 10000, 15000};
        System.out.println("Ceiling of 5000: index " + findCeiling(slabs, 5000)
                + " value " + slabs[findCeiling(slabs, 5000)]);
        System.out.println("Ceiling of 8000: index " + findCeiling(slabs, 8000));
        System.out.println("Ceiling of 20000: " + findCeiling(slabs, 20000));
    }
}
```

```text
Ceiling of 5000: index 2 value 8000
Ceiling of 8000: index 2
Ceiling of 20000: -1
```

### Why does the start pointer land on the ceiling?

During binary search, every time `start` moves right it discards an element smaller than the key. Every time `end` moves left it keeps elements at or above the key. When the loop ends with `start > end`, `start` is the first position where `arr[start] >= key`, which is the ceiling.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class CeilingConvergence {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] arr = {1000, 3000, 8000, 10000, 15000};
        int key = 5000;
        int start = 0, end = arr.length - 1;

        while (start <= end) {
            int mid = start + (end - start) / 2;
            System.out.println("start=" + start + " end=" + end + " mid=" + mid
                    + " arr[mid]=" + arr[mid]);
            if (arr[mid] == key) { System.out.println("Found!"); return; }
            if (key < arr[mid]) end = mid - 1;
            else start = mid + 1;
        }
        System.out.println("Ceiling at start=" + start + ": " + arr[start]);
    }
}
```

```text
start=0 end=4 mid=2 arr[mid]=8000
start=0 end=1 mid=0 arr[mid]=1000
start=1 end=1 mid=1 arr[mid]=3000
Ceiling at start=2: 8000
```

### What is the floor and how does it relate?

The floor of a key is the largest element less than or equal to the key. It is the mirror of the ceiling. When binary search ends, `end` points to the floor index. Knowing both invariants, start-on-ceiling and end-on-floor, gives complete command of the pattern.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class FloorOfNumber {

    public static int findFloor(int[] arr, int key) {
        if (key < arr[0]) return -1;

        int start = 0, end = arr.length - 1;
        while (start <= end) {
            int mid = start + (end - start) / 2;
            if (arr[mid] == key) return mid;
            if (key < arr[mid]) end = mid - 1;
            else start = mid + 1;
        }
        return end;  // end points to the floor
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] slabs = {1000, 3000, 8000, 10000, 15000};
        int floorIdx = findFloor(slabs, 5000);
        System.out.println("Floor of 5000: index " + floorIdx + " value " + slabs[floorIdx]);
    }
}
```

```text
Floor of 5000: index 1 value 3000
```

### How is ceiling used in slab-based lookups?

In banking, interest rates are assigned by slabs. Given a loan amount, the ceiling slab determines the applicable rate. This is a direct application of the ceiling algorithm.

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class InterestSlabLookup {

    public static int findCeiling(int[] slabs, int amount) {
        if (amount > slabs[slabs.length - 1]) return -1;
        int start = 0, end = slabs.length - 1;
        while (start <= end) {
            int mid = start + (end - start) / 2;
            if (slabs[mid] == amount) return mid;
            if (amount < slabs[mid]) end = mid - 1;
            else start = mid + 1;
        }
        return start;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] slabs = {50000, 100000, 500000, 1000000};
        BigDecimal[] rates = {
            new BigDecimal("8.5"), new BigDecimal("9.0"),
            new BigDecimal("10.5"), new BigDecimal("12.0")
        };

        int loanAmount = 75000;
        int slabIdx = findCeiling(slabs, loanAmount);
        System.out.println("Loan: " + loanAmount + " → Slab: " + slabs[slabIdx]
                + " Rate: " + rates[slabIdx] + "%");
    }
}
```

```text
Loan: 75000 → Slab: 100000 Rate: 9.0%
```

### What are the edge cases?

Edge cases include the key being smaller than the first element (ceiling is the first element), the key matching an element exactly, and the key exceeding the last element.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class CeilingEdgeCases {

    public static int findCeiling(int[] arr, int key) {
        if (key > arr[arr.length - 1]) return -1;
        int start = 0, end = arr.length - 1;
        while (start <= end) {
            int mid = start + (end - start) / 2;
            if (arr[mid] == key) return mid;
            if (key < arr[mid]) end = mid - 1;
            else start = mid + 1;
        }
        return start;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] arr = {1000, 3000, 8000, 10000};
        System.out.println("Below min (500): " + findCeiling(arr, 500));
        System.out.println("Exact match (3000): " + findCeiling(arr, 3000));
        System.out.println("Above max (20000): " + findCeiling(arr, 20000));
        System.out.println("Between (5000): " + findCeiling(arr, 5000));
    }
}
```

```text
Below min (500): 0
Exact match (3000): 1
Above max (20000): -1
Between (5000): 2
```

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is forgetting the edge case where the key exceeds the last element, causing `start` to go out of bounds. Always check this before the loop.

### How does this relate to Java's Arrays.binarySearch?

Java's `Arrays.binarySearch` returns `-(insertion point) - 1` when the key is not found. The insertion point is exactly the ceiling index. So `int ip = -(result) - 1` gives the same answer as our ceiling algorithm.

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.Arrays;

public class JavaBinarySearchCeiling {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] arr = {1000, 3000, 8000, 10000, 15000};
        int result = Arrays.binarySearch(arr, 5000);
        if (result >= 0) {
            System.out.println("Found at: " + result);
        } else {
            int insertionPoint = -(result) - 1;
            System.out.println("Ceiling index: " + insertionPoint
                    + " value: " + arr[insertionPoint]);
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Ceiling index: 2 value: 8000
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer should understand both the start-on-ceiling and end-on-floor invariants, explain why each movement preserves or discards candidates, and know that Java's built-in `Arrays.binarySearch` encodes the ceiling position in its negative return value. Recognizing these invariants is the key insight for the Modified Binary Search pattern.
